\section{Accelerated Information Propagation in Bosonic Lattices}\label{30005663}

\subsection{Definitions and mathematical statement}
\paragraph{Shared notation.}$\mathbb N=\{1,2,\ldots\}$; the standard number fields have their usual meanings. All additional conventions are stated locally.


A bosonic lattice has unbounded local occupation numbers. For local observables $A$ and $B$, propagation can be measured by $[A(t),B]$, where $A(t)=e^{itH}Ae^{-itH}$. An effective light cone bounds the spatial range where this commutator can be appreciable. Acceleration means faster spreading than a uniform finite-velocity cone in the source's state-dependent setting.

\paragraph{Catalogue wording retained for traceability.}
When does accelerated information propagation occur in bosonic lattice systems with nearest-neighbor or long-range interactions?

\paragraph{Scope and source qualification.}The allowed states and interaction decay matter because local bosonic operators are unbounded. Both nearest-neighbor and long-range interactions remain within the question.

\subsection{Short English statement}
Identify when information in bosonic lattices propagates faster than a uniform finite-speed light cone.

\subsection{Research attempt}
\paragraph{Model, date and investigated scope.}
OpenAI GPT-6 Astra (configured model \texttt{gpt-6-astra}), ultra reasoning; generated 11 October 2026. Solved two-site hopping exactly to reject matrix-element growth alone as evidence of accelerated information transfer.

\paragraph{Sources and literature check.}
Read the full supplied definition, catalogue wording, source qualifications and listed references for 30005663. No fresh status or novelty search was performed. The argument below proves its auxiliary statements directly and does not import an unverified external theorem.

\paragraph{Large hopping matrix elements do not alone prove acceleration.} In a two-site free bosonic system let \(a_j,a_j^*\) obey \([a_i,a_j^*]=\delta_{ij}\) and take \(H=J(a_1^*a_2+a_2^*a_1)\) with real \(J\ne0\). On occupation vectors the transition matrix element from \(|n,n\rangle\) to \(|n+1,n-1\rangle\) is \(J\sqrt{n(n+1)}\). This grows without bound, tempting an occupation-dependent speed conclusion.
\paragraph{Exact countercheck.} The Heisenberg equations instead close:
\[
 \dot a_1(t)=-iJ a_2(t),\qquad
 a_1(t)=\cos(Jt)a_1-i\sin(Jt)a_2.
\]
The analogous formula exchanges the indices. In the state \(|N,0\rangle\), with \(N\geq1\), one obtains
\[
 \langle a_2^*(t)a_2(t)\rangle=N\sin^2(Jt).
\]
The fraction transferred and the first complete transfer time \(\pi/(2|J|)\) are independent of \(N\). This follows directly by substituting the linear solution; no unbounded-operator norm estimate is taken. The finite-particle sectors justify the differentiations.
\paragraph{Remaining propagation problem.} These two sites provide no spatial light-cone theorem. They show that increasing individual occupation-basis matrix elements is insufficient evidence for accelerated transmission of a fixed fraction of a signal. The source question still requires admissible state classes, a quantitative information observable and spatially extended dynamics; interactions or long-range hopping may change the conclusion. Neither the nearest-neighbor nor the long-range source regime is classified by this free calculation.

\paragraph{Verification and adversarial audit.}
Checked occupation square roots, the Heisenberg signs, finite-sector domains and N-independent fractional transfer time; no operator-norm claim for unbounded fields. Model self-audit only; no independent human mathematical verification. Applied contribution prompt A before drafting and B afterwards. Checked occupation square roots, the Heisenberg signs, finite-sector domains and N-independent fractional transfer time; no operator-norm claim for unbounded fields. First remaining gap: Construct and control spatially extended state-dependent signals in the source nearest-neighbor and long-range interacting models. The inherited source status is preserved; original-target completion remains zero.

